---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import AuthorByline from '../components/AuthorByline.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Your First Medicare Decisions and Beyond';
const description = 'When to enroll in Parts A and B, then how to handle the costs Medicare leaves: Medigap or High-Deductible Medigap plus Part D, or Medicare Advantage.';
const today = '2026-09-30';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    { '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description, author: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#agent` }, publisher: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#organization` }, datePublished: today, dateModified: today, mainEntityOfPage: `${BUSINESS.url}/your-first-medicare-decisions` },
    { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/your-first-medicare-decisions#webpage`, name: title, speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['.lead', 'h2'] } },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <PageHero
    title="Your First Medicare Decisions and Beyond"
    subtitle="When to enroll in Original Medicare, then how to handle the costs and coverage it doesn’t provide by itself."
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Your First Medicare Decisions and Beyond' }]}
  />

  <article class="py-14 lg:py-18">
    <div class="container-narrow prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-crst-primary prose-a:text-crst-accent">

      <p class="lead text-xl text-navy-700">
        As you approach Medicare eligibility, your first step is determining when to enroll in Medicare Part A and Part B.
        Those two Parts are often referred to as Original Medicare. Once you have Original Medicare, your next decision is
        how you want to handle the costs and coverage that Medicare doesn’t provide by itself.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-500 -mt-2">
        Written by <a href="/about">Jim Farnham, MS, MBA</a> — Licensed Agent / Insurance Broker (NY license {BUSINESS.nyLicense}).
      </p>

      <p>
        I help you evaluate High-Deductible Medicare Supplement Insurance alongside traditional Medigap, Part D and Medicare
        Advantage — so you can choose what truly fits your situation.
      </p>

      <p>You generally have two paths:</p>

      <h2>Option 1: Original Medicare + Medigap/High Deductible Medigap + Part D</h2>
      <p>
        Stay with Original Medicare (Parts A and B), then add a Medicare Supplement Insurance Plan (Medigap) to help with
        Medicare’s deductibles and cost sharing and choose a Part D Prescription Drug Plan for medication coverage. For more
        info, see our <a href="/turning-65">Turning 65 and Medicare</a> and
        <a href="/services/high-deductible-medicare-supplement">High-Deductible Medicare Supplement Insurance</a> pages.
      </p>

      <p class="text-center font-heading font-semibold text-crst-primary">or</p>

      <h2>Option 2: Medicare Advantage (Part C)</h2>
      <p>
        Choose a Medicare Advantage Plan offered by a private insurance company as an alternative way to receive your
        Medicare benefits. Most Medicare Advantage plans include Part D prescription drug coverage and may include additional
        benefits. For more info, see our <a href="/services/medicare-advantage">Medicare Advantage Plans (Part C)</a> page.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-white font-heading text-lg mb-2 m-0">Not sure which path fits you?</p>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mb-4 m-0">A no-cost, no-pressure conversation by phone or Zoom screen share.</p>
        <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-base px-7 py-3">Call {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
      </div>

      <h2>Medigap Rules Are Different Depending on Where You Live</h2>
      <p>
        Although Medigap policies are federally standardized, the rules governing when you can enroll—or change plans—vary
        from state to state. Understanding those differences can have a significant impact on which Medigap plan is the best
        long-term choice.
      </p>

      <h3>New York and Connecticut</h3>
      <p>
        Residents of New York and Connecticut enjoy one of the strongest consumer protections in the country. After your
        initial Medigap Open Enrollment Period ends, you generally continue to have guaranteed issue rights. That means you
        can usually change from one Medigap plan to another without being denied coverage because of your health or being
        charged more due to pre-existing medical conditions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because changing plans remains an option later, you may have greater flexibility if your healthcare needs change
        over time.
      </p>

      <h3>Most Other States</h3>
      <p>
        In most other states, everyone receives the same important protection during their one-time Medigap Open Enrollment
        Period, which begins when you are both age 65 or older and enrolled in Medicare Part B.
      </p>
      <p>During that six-month period:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Insurance companies cannot deny your application because of your health.</li>
        <li>They cannot charge you a higher premium because of pre-existing conditions.</li>
        <li>They cannot impose waiting periods for pre-existing conditions.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>However, after that enrollment period ends, the rules are different.</p>
      <p>
        If you later decide to switch Medigap plans, insurance companies in most states are generally allowed to review your
        health history through medical underwriting. Depending on your health, your application may be approved, declined,
        or offered under different terms.
      </p>

      <h3>Why This Matters</h3>
      <p>
        Your state’s Medigap rules can influence which plan makes the most sense—not just today, but years from now.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, in states where changing plans later may require medical underwriting, some beneficiaries choose to
        purchase more comprehensive coverage initially because they may not be able to qualify for it later if their health
        changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the other hand, some beneficiaries may find that a High-Deductible Medigap Plan G is an appropriate fit. It
        typically has a lower monthly premium, while still providing the protection of a Medigap policy after the annual
        deductible has been met.
      </p>
      <p>Whether a high-deductible plan is appropriate depends on factors such as:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your current health</li>
        <li>How often you typically use medical services</li>
        <li>Your comfort with paying a larger deductible in exchange for lower monthly premiums</li>
        <li>The Medigap enrollment rules in your state</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Choosing the Right Strategy</h3>
      <p>There is no single Medigap plan that is best for everyone.</p>
      <p>
        The right choice depends on your health, your budget, your expected healthcare usage, and the enrollment rules where
        you live. Understanding how your state’s rules affect your future options can help you make a more informed decision.
      </p>

      <AuthorByline />

      <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <Disclaimer variant="tpmo" />
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          This page is educational and general; plan benefits, costs, and availability vary by insurer and location and are not
          guaranteed. NY producer license #: {BUSINESS.nyLicense}.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          Source: <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/health-drug-plans/medigap" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Medicare.gov — Medicare Supplement (Medigap)</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection headline="New to Medicare? Let’s work through your first decisions together." />
</BaseLayout>
